### Introduction

`:=` declares a variable and infers a type from its right-hand side, then locks that identifier to the inferred type. Every later plain `=` assignment is checked against the locked type, raising `Disk::Type_Contract_Violation` on a mismatch -- this is a runtime check, separate from the static type checker's own `Type_Mismatch`.
###
survivors := 48   # locks survivors to Number
survivors = 47     # ok -- still a Number

@assert survivors == 47, 'a same-type reassignment should be allowed'

# survivors = 'forty-seven'  -- raises Disk::Type_Contract_Violation


### Reusing := on the same variable redeclares it, and relocks the type

Declaring the same identifier again with `:=`, instead of `=`, re-infers its type from whatever's on the right this time and overwrites the lock -- no error, even though the type actually changed.
###
status := 48
status := 'stranded'   # fine -- := re-infers and re-locks

@assert status == 'stranded', 're-declaring with := should relock the type to the new value'


### = requires a prior declaration

Plain `=` can only assign to an identifier that's already been declared -- by `:=`, by a `: Type` annotation, or by one of the other self-declaring forms. Assigning to a name that's never been declared at all raises `Disk::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier`.
###
# raft_ready = true  -- raises Disk::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier, raft_ready was never declared


### := always declares fresh on the current scope

Unlike `=`, which resolves outward to find where an identifier already lives, `:=` always declares on the scope it's written in -- shadowing any identically-named identifier further out, rather than reusing it.
###
counter := 0

increment (;
	counter := 99   # shadows -- a brand new local, the outer counter is untouched
	counter += 1    # mutates the local shadow, still 100
)

increment()
@assert counter == 0, ':= inside a function should shadow, never touch the outer counter'
